<Layout
	title="About us"
	description="Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet"
	pubDate={new Date('October 08 2026')}
	heroImage={AboutHeroImage}
>
	<p>
		Baseball is notoriously unpredictable, with momentum shifting at any moment during a game. With this idea,
		we <strong>intend to capture what is known and discover part of what remains unknown.</strong> I am personally
		proud of our ability to predict attendance at ballparks, helping management prepare and adjust pricing for
		improved revenue. It is also an amazing opportunity to map out and deploy code that can be used by the masses.
		<strong>- Rodolfo, Website Administrator</strong>
	</p>

	<p>
		Our project is unique because it combines MLB attendance, ballpark characteristics, weather, scheduling,
		and matchup data into one interactive analytics application. Rather than simply displaying baseball statistics,
		it addresses a real-world business problem by identifying which game conditions may create higher demand for
		tickets. The project <strong>would be useful to teams for planning promotions, evaluating attendance trends, and
		supporting ticket-pricing decisions</strong>, while also demonstrating practical database design, query optimization,
		and web application development.
		<strong>- Kyle, Frontend Development</strong>
	</p>

	<p>
		Some baseball games begin with half the stadium still empty. Those unsold tickets represent revenue the stadium
		will not recoup. We hope to assist ticketing departments by estimating the value of tickets based on data that
		makes one ticket more valuable than another. Factors such as weather, opponent quality, day of the week, and the
		stadium itself all affect the value of a seat. In our project, we want to create an application 
		that <strong>provides an estimate of attendance for a game and correlates that estimate with the value of each seat in a stadium.</strong>
		<strong>- Jimmy, Quality Assurance</strong>
	</p>

	<p>
		Sports, when viewed as a business, are always on the cutting edge of technology and analytics, which surprises
		people because they do not see their favorite team or league as a business. The MLB pulls in more than $12
		billion every year, and the reason it manages to do so is based on using algorithms to raise prices for tickets,
		concessions, merchandise, parking, and more as high as possible without decreasing attendance by pricing people
		out. This idea is further supported by private equity being allowed to buy stakes in team ownership as of 2019,
		the first major American professional sports league to permit private equity investment directly in its
		franchises. <strong>Our project aims to develop something similar to what teams and investors use to project
		attendance statistics and potentially estimate ticket prices as well.</strong>
		<strong>- Grant, Data Acquisition and Deployment</strong>
	</p>

	
</Layout>
